+// license:BSD-3-Clause
+// copyright-holders:psmac project
+/****************************************************************************
+
+    bridge68k.cpp
+
+    "External CPU" placeholder for the psmac software testbed (see the header
+    and docs/05-bridge-protocol.md).
+
+    Timing model
+    ------------
+    * Every READ/WRITE served over the socket is charged ACCESS_CYCLES (4)
+      cycles of the device clock (25 MHz on the IIci).
+    * When no request is pending the device consumes cycles proportional to
+      the wall-clock time that has elapsed (osd_ticks()), so emulated time
+      follows real time and VIA timers / 60 Hz video / ADB keep running even
+      with -nothrottle.  A single catch-up is capped at 10 ms worth of cycles.
+    * Idle waiting is done in poll() with a 1 ms timeout, so the host CPU is
+      not spun needlessly.
+
+    Bus error / DTACK model
+    -----------------------
+    MAME delivers input-line changes through the scheduler (set_input_line()
+    is synchronised), so a M68K_LINE_BUSERROR pulse raised by a handler during
+    an access is only visible to this device after execute_run() has returned
+    once.  Therefore the reply for a READ/WRITE is not sent immediately: the
+    access is performed, execute_run() returns, and the reply (with the bus
+    error status) goes out at the start of the next execute_run() call.  If
+    the access made the motherboard hold the CPU (INPUT_LINE_HALT, e.g. the
+    SCSI pseudo-DMA FIFO fill), the base class suspends the device and the
+    reply is naturally delayed until the hold ends: that is the DTACK wait.
+
+****************************************************************************/
+
+#include "emu.h"
+#include "bridge68k.h"
