+// license:BSD-3-Clause
+// copyright-holders:R. Belmont, psmac project
+/****************************************************************************
+
+    macse_ps.cpp
+
+    Macintosh SE with the 68000 replaced by the psmac bridge CPU
+    (bridge68000_device): the testbed for Aurora68's board layer.
+
+    Derived from the SE parts of mac128.cpp by R. Belmont.  All peripherals
+    are the stock SE's (VIA, SCC, IWM, NCR 5380 through the SCSI helper,
+    ADB through the ADB modem, the RTC, the 512 by 342 screen and the PWM
+    sound both fed from RAM); only the CPU differs: bus cycles are served
+    from an external emulator over the bridge's FIFO (bridge68k.h,
+    docs/05-bridge-protocol.md), 16 bits wide on a 24-bit bus, exactly what
+    a classic PiStorm in the SE's 68000 socket would present.
+
+    What this machine is for: the IIci ROM, running on the Pi side, drives
+    IIci hardware; the kernel's board layer (docs/12) must translate that
+    into the SE's address map and emulate what the SE lacks.  Everything the
+    kernel sends down the wire lands here as it would on the real logic
+    board, so the translation can be debugged before the hardware arrives.
+
+    Deviations from mac128.cpp's macse:
+    - no m68000-specific hooks (VPA synchronisation on the VIA, the
+      disassembler override, trigger_bus_error): the bridge device takes
+      the bus-error pulse on its LINE_BUSERROR input like the IIci bridge;
+    - the overlay (ROM at 0 after reset) is cleared by the first access to
+      the ROM's own address space, as the SE's BBU does, and also by the
+      first write to RAM, as mac128.cpp does; the external CPU never
+      fetches from the SE's ROM, so the kernel reads $400000 once at start;
+    - no PDS slot (nothing to put in it in the testbed), no software lists;
+    - ADB without the SE's 6805 "ADB modem" (its ROM is not in the testbed's
+      set): the keyboard and mouse hang off the VIA's shift register and
+      port B state bits directly, the way maciici_ps.cpp wires them, which is
+      also exactly how the IIci ROM drives ADB.
+
+****************************************************************************/
+
